@ id: lbq02
@ order: 602
@ title: The mechanism — the BitLinear layer
@ subtitle: absmean ternary weight quantization, 8-bit activations, the STE, and where LayerNorm goes
@ source: arxiv 2402.17764

--- note
## One drop-in replacement: nn.Linear → BitLinear
BitNet is architecturally an ordinary Transformer with **one substitution**: every `nn.Linear`
(the parametric projections — Q/K/V, attention output, and the FFN matrices) is replaced by a
**`BitLinear`** layer. Everything else is left high-precision: the residual connections, the input/
output token embeddings, and the LayerNorms. The paper justifies this: residuals and norms are
negligible compute; the embedding must stay precise because the model needs high-precision output
**probabilities** for sampling; and as the model grows, the linear projections dominate cost — so
quantizing exactly those is where the savings live.

--- image
learning/assets/bitnet/bitnet_fig2_bitlinear.png
Figure 2 (2310.11453). LEFT: the computation flow *inside* BitLinear, bottom-to-top — Input → **LayerNorm** → **Absmax Quantization** of activations → multiply by the **1-bit (here ternary) weights** → **Dequantization** rescaling by the stored scales (β, γ) → Output. RIGHT: a BitNet block is a normal Transformer — multi-head attention + feed-forward — with every matmul (Q, K, V, attention-out, and the two FFN matrices) drawn as a `BitLinear`.

--- note
## Step 1 — quantize the WEIGHTS to ternary (absmean)
BitNet b1.58 constrains each weight to {-1, 0, +1} with an **absmean** quantization function. The recipe:
compute the **mean absolute value** of the whole weight matrix, use it as a scale γ, divide every weight
by that scale, and **round to the nearest integer, clipped to [-1, +1]**:

- **γ = (1/nm) · Σ|W_ij|** — the average magnitude of the matrix (n·m entries).
- **W̃ = RoundClip( W / (γ + ε), −1, 1 )**, where **RoundClip(x, a, b) = max(a, min(b, round(x)))**.

Weights near zero round to **0** (the new ternary state); larger-magnitude weights round to **±1**. The
single scalar γ (plus a small ε for stability) captures the matrix's overall magnitude so the ternary
weights only have to encode the **sign-and-presence** pattern.

--- image
learning/assets/bitnet/b158_absmean_eqs.png
The absmean weight-quantization equations, verbatim from §2 of 2402.17764: Eq (1) W̃ = RoundClip(W/(γ+ε), −1, 1); Eq (2) RoundClip(x,a,b) = max(a, min(b, round(x))); Eq (3) γ = (1/nm) Σ|W_ij|. Note this differs from the *original* BitNet (2310.11453), whose weights were binary via W̃ = Sign(W − α) — b1.58's contribution is the ternary absmean rule that admits the value 0.

--- note
## Step 2 — quantize the ACTIVATIONS to 8-bit (absmax)
Weights are ternary, but the **activations** flowing between layers are quantized to **8-bit**. The
original BitNet uses **absmax** quantization: scale activations into the range [−Qb, Qb] (with
Qb = 2^(b−1), so Qb = 128 for b = 8) by dividing by the per-tensor maximum absolute value γ = ‖x‖∞ and
multiplying by Qb, then clip. b1.58 simplifies one detail from the original: it scales **all** activations
to **[−Qb, Qb] per token** and drops the special "shift to [0, Qb] before non-linearities" step, which
**removes the zero-point** — simpler for implementation and system-level optimization, with negligible
effect on quality. The paper notes activation quantization is done **per-tensor during training** (for
efficiency) but **per-token during inference** (for accuracy). This is a **W1.58A8** scheme: ~1.58-bit
weights, 8-bit activations.

--- note
## Step 3 — the STE: how gradients flow through a non-differentiable round
`round`, `clip`, and `sign` are **flat or undefined** almost everywhere — their derivative is 0, which
would kill training. BitNet trains through them with the **Straight-Through Estimator (STE)** (Bengio et
al., 2013): in the **forward** pass you quantize (round/clip) as above; in the **backward** pass you
**pretend the quantizer was the identity** and pass the gradient straight through, unchanged. This lets
gradients reach the underlying real-valued weights even though the forward pass only ever used the
ternary versions. The STE is the single most important trick that makes low-bit **training** possible —
and it is exactly what our `lowbit-qat` fleet agent implements.

--- note
## Step 4 — mixed precision & the "latent" weights
The quantization is a **forward-pass simulation** — the model does not literally store ternary weights
during training. Following the original BitNet: a **high-precision "latent" weight** is kept for every
parameter, and the **gradients and optimizer states stay in high precision** too (for stability). Each
forward pass **quantizes the latent weight on the fly** to ternary; the update is accumulated on the
high-precision latent copy. Only at **inference** are the weights frozen to ternary — the latent copy is
"never used for the inference process." This is what "quantization-**aware** training" means concretely:
the network learns *with* the rounding in the loop, but keeps a smooth parameter to descend on.

--- note
## Step 5 — LayerNorm placement (SubLN) keeps the variance sane
Quantizing activations changes their variance, which can destabilize training. The original BitNet fixes
this by inserting a **LayerNorm before the activation quantization** (its "SubLN" placement, shown at the
bottom of the BitLinear flow above), chosen so the **output variance stays ≈ 1** — matching what a
full-precision layer with standard (Kaiming/Xavier) initialization would produce. The full BitLinear
forward is then `y = W̃ · Quant(LN(x)) · (βγ / Qb)`, where the output is **dequantized** by rescaling with
the stored scales β (the weight scale, (1/nm)‖W‖₁) and γ (the activation scale). The ablation in lbq06
shows SubLN beats the Pre-LN and BMT alternatives.

--- note
## LLaMA-alike components (so it drops into real stacks)
To be usable in the open-source ecosystem, BitNet b1.58 adopts the **LLaMA** component set around the
BitLinear layers: **RMSNorm**, **SwiGLU** activations, **rotary position embeddings (RoPE)**, and it
**removes all biases**. The paper states this lets it integrate into HuggingFace, vLLM, and llama.cpp
"with minimal efforts."
